#!/usr/bin/env python3
# requires: python3 rofi systemctl

"""Decide what the desktop starts with, and what is running right now.

    startup                     pick a component from the launcher
    startup --list              what is optional, its state, and what it costs
    startup --current           the same, one line per component, for scripts
    startup waybar off          off now and off at the next login
    startup waybar on           on now and on at the next login
    startup mako --now off      stop it now, still starts at the next login
    startup mako --autostart off   next login only, leave it running now

TWO AXES, BECAUSE THEY ARE DIFFERENT QUESTIONS

"Do I want the bar at all" and "get the bar out of my way for ten minutes" are
not the same request, and a single on/off answers only one of them. systemd
already separates them - a unit is enabled or not, and active or not - so this
keeps that separation rather than flattening it. Every combination is reachable,
including the awkward one: switched off at login and running right now.

WHAT IS OFFERED, AND WHAT DELIBERATELY IS NOT

Only components whose absence is *visible and recoverable*. If a thing can fail
in a way you would not notice, it does not belong here at any memory saving -
see NOT_OFFERED below, which is the interesting half of this file.

HOW "AT LOGIN" IS SWITCHED OFF, AND WHY IT IS NOT `systemctl --user disable`

This is the whole difficulty, and the obvious answer does not work.

The session components are pulled in by symlinks in
~/.config/systemd/user/wayland-session@sway.target.wants/, and **chezmoi ships
those symlinks**. So `systemctl --user disable mako` deletes a file chezmoi
manages, and the next ./sync.sh puts it back - a toggle that silently reverts,
which is worse than no toggle. Measured, rather than assumed: after the symlink
is removed, `chezmoi apply` does not quietly recreate it, it *prompts*

    .config/.../wayland-session@sway.target.wants/mako.service has changed
    since chezmoi last wrote it?

on every single run, forever, and sync.sh's non-interactive path fails outright
with --error-on-conflict. So `disable` is not merely undone; it makes syncing
the machine worse.

Three other routes were tried on this machine and rejected, each because it
produced a confident-looking wrong result rather than an error:

  * A `Wants=` reset in a drop-in on wayland-session@sway.target. The reset
    syntax is real and this repository already uses it for `After=` in waybar's
    override - but symlink dependencies are added *after* the fragments are
    parsed, so the reset does not clear them. `systemctl show -p Wants` before
    and after the drop-in prints exactly the same list. It looks like a
    configured toggle and does nothing at all.

  * `systemctl --user mask`. A user mask lands in ~/.config/systemd/user/, which
    is precisely where chezmoi writes five of these six unit files; and it also
    blocks starting the unit by hand, which collapses the two axes into one.

  * `mask --runtime`, which lands in $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR and is gone by the next
    boot, so it cannot express "at login" at all.

What is used instead is a drop-in chezmoi does not know about:

    ~/.config/systemd/user/<unit>.d/50-startup-off.conf

carrying a condition that is false at login. chezmoi manages files, not
directories-with-anything-in-them: an unmanaged file inside a managed directory
is invisible to `chezmoi status` and `chezmoi apply` (checked, by putting one in
waybar.service.d and looking). So the symlink stays exactly as the repository
wrote it, nothing ever conflicts, ./sync.sh stays quiet - and the unit still
does not start.

There is exactly one thing this costs, and it is written down here rather than
discovered later: `systemctl --user is-enabled mako` still says "enabled",
because the symlink really is still there. `startup --list` reports the
effective state instead, read from systemd's own view of the drop-in
(`systemctl --user cat` shows it, and `status` says "start condition unmet"), so
there is no private database anywhere - but the systemctl one-liner will
disagree with reality until the follow-up below is done.

THE FOLLOW-UP THAT REMOVES THAT WART

Two lines in setup/dotfiles/.chezmoiignore - which already exists to hold
exactly this kind of escape hatch, and says so - would make chezmoi stop
managing the .wants symlink of a component recorded as off, at which point
`systemctl --user disable` becomes safe and `is-enabled` tells the truth. That
is a change to a tracked file and belongs with the repository, not here.

WHY THE MARKER FILE EXISTS

A condition is evaluated for *every* start job, a manual one included - measured
on a throwaway unit: `systemctl --user start` on a condition-blocked unit exits
0 and leaves it inactive, silently. So switching a component off at login would
also make it unstartable, which is the collapse of the two axes this file exists
to avoid. The condition therefore names a marker under $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR that
this script creates for the length of one start job and removes again. A marker
left behind by a killed process would let the component start at the next login;
every run of this script sweeps stray markers first, and --list reports one if
it finds it, so the failure is loud rather than invisible.
"""

import os
import pathlib
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# What may be switched off
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The rule is the one the ticket asked for: optional means the desktop keeps
# working and you can always get back. A component is listed here only once
# someone has answered "what breaks, and how would I notice" - which is the
# second string.
#
# Anything wanted by wayland-session@sway.target and named in neither table is
# reported by --list as unclassified rather than silently offered or silently
# hidden, so a component added later has to be thought about once.

OPTIONAL = {
    "waybar": (
        "the bar: clock, tray, workspaces, click actions",
        "no bar. Every click action has a keybinding, and $mod+Shift+s and "
        "friends are unaffected",
    ),
    "autotiling": (
        "picks the sway split direction to suit the window shape",
        "new windows split the way sway would on its own. The manual split "
        "bindings are unchanged",
    ),
    "mako": (
        "notification popups",
        "notifications are not shown. Nothing else depends on it",
    ),
    "swayidle": (
        "blanks and locks the screen when idle",
        "the screen never blanks or locks itself. Note the direction of that "
        "risk: an unattended session stays unlocked. swaylock by hand is "
        "unaffected",
    ),
}

NOT_OFFERED = {
    "polkit-agent": (
        "Its absence is invisible. Without an agent a graphical privilege "
        "prompt does not appear and the operation quietly does nothing - the "
        "exact failure mode this setup keeps hitting, and sudo in a terminal "
        "still working is what stops you noticing. It costs about 6M, less "
        "than a quarter of the bar."
    ),
    "wayland-session-waitenv": (
        "uwsm's own plumbing, not a feature. It is what makes the session "
        "target wait until the compositor's environment has been exported, so "
        "switching it off would start every other component against an empty "
        "environment. It exits as soon as that is done and costs nothing."
    ),
}

# Things the ticket also asked about, answered here so the answer is findable.
#
#   NetworkManager - a system unit. Switching it off needs root, and on a
#   machine on wifi it is exactly the "cannot get back" case the ticket rules
#   out. Out of scope for a user-session tool.
#
#   pipewire / pipewire-pulse / wireplumber - genuinely optional and genuinely
#   user scope, but socket-activated and not part of this target, so "off"
#   means the sockets too and anything that touches audio turns them back on.
#   A different mechanism with a different failure mode; it needs its own
#   decision rather than being smuggled in here.

TARGET = "wayland-session@sway.target"
UNIT_DIR = pathlib.Path.home() / ".config" / "systemd" / "user"
DROPIN = "50-startup-off.conf"
MARKERS = pathlib.Path(
    os.environ.get("XDG_RUNTIME_DIR") or f"/run/user/{os.getuid()}"
) / "startup"


def die(message):
    print(f"startup: {message}", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(1)


def systemctl(*args, check=True):
    return subprocess.run(
        ["systemctl", "--user", *args],
        capture_output=True, text=True, check=False,
    )


def show(unit, prop):
    return systemctl("show", unit, "-p", prop, "--value").stdout.strip()


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# State
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

def unit_name(component):
    return f"{component}.service"


def dropin_path(component):
    return UNIT_DIR / f"{unit_name(component)}.d" / DROPIN


def marker_path(component):
    return MARKERS / unit_name(component)


def wanted_components():
    """What the session target actually pulls in, asked of systemd.

    Not a hardcoded list, because components get added - cliphist and the
    workspace greeter both arrived after this target was first written - and a
    tool that answers "what starts at login" from a list of its own would start
    lying the moment one did.
    """
    wants = show(TARGET, "Wants").split()
    if not wants:
        # The target is not loaded (no graphical session). Fall back to the
        # directory the symlinks live in, which is the same information.
        wants = [p.name for p in (UNIT_DIR / f"{TARGET}.wants").glob("*.service")]
    return sorted({w[: -len(".service")] for w in wants if w.endswith(".service")})


def autostart_of(component):
    return "off" if dropin_path(component).exists() else "on"


def now_of(component):
    return "on" if systemctl(
        "is-active", "--quiet", unit_name(component)
    ).returncode == 0 else "off"


def memory_of(component):
    raw = show(unit_name(component), "MemoryCurrent")
    if not raw.isdigit():
        return "-"
    value = int(raw)
    for suffix in ("K", "M", "G"):
        value /= 1024
        if value < 1024:
            return f"{value:.1f}{suffix}"
    return f"{value:.1f}T"


def sweep_markers():
    """Remove markers left behind by an interrupted run.

    A marker only ever lives for the duration of one `systemctl start`. One
    found at the start of a run is debris, and debris here means a component
    switched off at login would quietly start anyway - so it is cleared, and
    --list says so.
    """
    stray = []
    if MARKERS.is_dir():
        for path in MARKERS.iterdir():
            stray.append(path.name)
            path.unlink(missing_ok=True)
    return stray


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Changing it
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

DROPIN_TEXT = """\
# {component} does not start at login on this machine.
#
# Written by ~/.local/bin/startup - see that file for why this is a drop-in and
# not `systemctl --user disable`. The short version: chezmoi ships the symlink
# that enables this unit, so disabling it is undone by the next ./sync.sh, and
# in the meantime makes `chezmoi apply` prompt on every run. chezmoi does not
# know about this file, so nothing fights over it.
#
# The condition is false at login, so the unit is skipped rather than failed.
# `startup {component} --now on` creates the marker for the length of one start
# job, which is how it can still be started by hand while staying off at login.
#
# Delete this file (and `systemctl --user daemon-reload`) to undo by hand, or
# run `startup {component} --autostart on`.

[Unit]
ConditionPathExists=%t/startup/{unit}
"""


def set_autostart(component, state):
    path = dropin_path(component)
    if state == "off":
        path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        path.write_text(DROPIN_TEXT.format(
            component=component, unit=unit_name(component)))
    else:
        path.unlink(missing_ok=True)
        # Only remove the directory if this script created it. waybar's is
        # shipped by the repository and holds override.conf.
        try:
            path.parent.rmdir()
        except OSError:
            pass
    result = systemctl("daemon-reload")
    if result.returncode != 0:
        die(f"daemon-reload failed: {result.stderr.strip()}")


def set_now(component, state):
    unit = unit_name(component)
    if state == "off":
        result = systemctl("stop", unit)
        if result.returncode != 0:
            die(f"could not stop {unit}: {result.stderr.strip()}")
        return

    # Starting is the case the condition gets in the way of. The marker is
    # created only when the drop-in is actually present, and removed again
    # whatever happens - `systemctl start` is synchronous, so by the time it
    # returns the condition has been evaluated and the marker has done its job.
    blocked = dropin_path(component).exists()
    if blocked:
        MARKERS.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        marker_path(component).touch()
    try:
        result = systemctl("start", unit)
    finally:
        if blocked:
            marker_path(component).unlink(missing_ok=True)
    if result.returncode != 0:
        die(f"could not start {unit}: {result.stderr.strip()}")
    # A start that was skipped exits 0, so the return code proves nothing.
    if now_of(component) != "on":
        die(f"{unit} did not start; see `systemctl --user status {unit}`")


def apply_change(component, autostart, now):
    # Order matters in one direction only: turning something on should not have
    # to wait for the next login to be visible, and turning it off should not
    # leave a running process behind.
    if autostart is not None:
        set_autostart(component, autostart)
    if now is not None and now != now_of(component):
        set_now(component, now)


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reporting
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

def classify():
    wanted = wanted_components()
    offered = [c for c in wanted if c in OPTIONAL]
    # A component can be switched off and still be listed here, because the
    # symlink is untouched - which is the point of the mechanism.
    withheld = [c for c in wanted if c in NOT_OFFERED]
    unknown = [c for c in wanted if c not in OPTIONAL and c not in NOT_OFFERED]
    return offered, withheld, unknown


def print_list(stray):
    offered, withheld, unknown = classify()
    print(f"{'COMPONENT':<12}  {'AT LOGIN':<8}  {'NOW':<3}  {'MEMORY':>6}  WHAT IT DOES")
    for component in offered:
        print(f"{component:<12}  {autostart_of(component):<8}  "
              f"{now_of(component):<3}  {memory_of(component):>6}  "
              f"{OPTIONAL[component][0]}")
    if withheld:
        print()
        print("Not offered, deliberately:")
        for component in withheld:
            print(f"  {component} ({memory_of(component)})")
            for line in wrap(NOT_OFFERED[component], 66):
                print(f"      {line}")
    if unknown:
        print()
        print("Wanted at login but not classified as optional or withheld.")
        print("Someone has to decide which it is; until then it is left alone:")
        for component in unknown:
            print(f"  {component} ({memory_of(component)})")
    if stray:
        print()
        print("Cleared a leftover start marker for: " + ", ".join(stray))
        print("Something interrupted a previous run. Nothing is wrong now.")


def wrap(text, width):
    lines, line = [], ""
    for word in text.split():
        if line and len(line) + 1 + len(word) > width:
            lines.append(line)
            line = word
        else:
            line = f"{line} {word}".strip()
    if line:
        lines.append(line)
    return lines


def print_current():
    offered, _, _ = classify()
    for component in offered:
        print(f"{component} autostart={autostart_of(component)} "
              f"now={now_of(component)}")


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The picker
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

def pick():
    """One row per component; choosing it flips the whole thing.

    Flat and one keypress, because the fine-grained "off at login but on now"
    case is rare enough to be worth typing and common enough to need to exist.
    The rows say what will happen rather than making you work it out.
    """
    offered, _, _ = classify()
    if not offered:
        die("nothing optional is running; is the sway session up?")

    rows, actions = [], []
    for component in offered:
        autostart, now = autostart_of(component), now_of(component)
        on = autostart == "on" or now == "on"
        state = f"{'starts at login' if autostart == 'on' else 'off at login'}, " \
                f"{'running' if now == 'on' else 'not running'}"
        rows.append(f"{component:<12}  {state:<34}  "
                    f"{'turn off' if on else 'turn on':<8}  {memory_of(component)}")
        actions.append((component, "off" if on else "on"))

    # The total goes in the prompt, not in -mesg. This machine's config.rasi
    # sets mainbox children to [inputbar, listview, textbox-footer] with no
    # message widget, so rofi accepts -mesg, reports nothing, and shows
    # nothing - which is how the first version of this looked configured and
    # displayed an empty line's worth of nothing. Checked in a screenshot.
    total = sum(int(raw) for raw in
                (show(unit_name(c), "MemoryCurrent") for c in offered)
                if raw.isdigit())
    prompt = f"Startup ({total / 1024 / 1024:.0f}M)"

    result = subprocess.run(
        ["rofi", "-dmenu", "-i", "-replace", "-p", prompt,
         "-format", "i", "-no-custom"],
        input="\n".join(rows), capture_output=True, text=True,
    )
    choice = result.stdout.strip()
    if not choice.isdigit():
        return
    component, state = actions[int(choice)]
    apply_change(component, state, state)


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Arguments
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

def usage():
    print(__doc__.split("TWO AXES")[0].strip(), file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(2)


def main():
    stray = sweep_markers()
    args = sys.argv[1:]

    if not args:
        if not shutil.which("rofi"):
            die("rofi is not installed; use --list")
        pick()
        return

    if args == ["--list"]:
        print_list(stray)
        return
    if args == ["--current"]:
        print_current()
        return
    if args[0].startswith("-"):
        usage()

    component, rest = args[0], args[1:]
    if component in NOT_OFFERED:
        print(f"startup: {component} is deliberately not switchable.",
              file=sys.stderr)
        for line in wrap(NOT_OFFERED[component], 70):
            print(f"  {line}", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(1)
    if component not in OPTIONAL:
        die(f"unknown component {component!r}. `startup --list` shows what there is")
    if component not in wanted_components():
        die(f"{component} is not part of {TARGET} on this machine")

    if len(rest) == 1 and rest[0] in ("on", "off"):
        apply_change(component, rest[0], rest[0])
    elif len(rest) == 2 and rest[0] == "--now" and rest[1] in ("on", "off"):
        apply_change(component, None, rest[1])
    elif len(rest) == 2 and rest[0] == "--autostart" and rest[1] in ("on", "off"):
        apply_change(component, rest[1], None)
    elif not rest:
        print(f"{component} autostart={autostart_of(component)} "
              f"now={now_of(component)}")
        return
    else:
        usage()

    print(f"{component} autostart={autostart_of(component)} "
          f"now={now_of(component)}")


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
